抵达时展现了他最具魅力的一面。他用阿拉伯语向纳赛尔打招呼。他们一起坐在沙发上，聊起了《古兰经》。当然，艾登在牛津大学时就用阿拉伯语读过《古兰经》。他们还交流了各自喜爱的阿拉伯谚语。偷走运河的人。是的，正好。绝不会有好下场。一个拥有胡须的男人时刻准备着。无论如何，艾登对纳赛尔说，听着，我想让你对我们的最新举措感兴趣。

69
00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:52.000
And this is a thing called the Baghdad Pact. The Baghdad Pact is a kind of NATO for the Middle East. So it will be Britain and it will be Iraq, Iran, Turkey and Pakistan. People can perhaps already see why this didn't become a tremendously successful lasting alliance. They will be together in a military alliance to contain the Soviet Union. And NASA says, I don't mind the sound of this. This sounds very much like you're trying to bring back the British end baron in a disguised form. But actually, the meeting isn't that bad.
这是一个叫做“巴格达条约”的东西。巴格达条约有点像是中东的北约。因此，它的成员将包括英国、伊拉克、伊朗、土耳其和巴基斯坦。人们或许已经能看出为什么这个联盟没有成为一个极其成功且持久的联盟。他们将结成军事联盟以遏制苏联。纳赛尔说，我对这个听起来没什么意见。这听起来很像是你们在试图以伪装的形式恢复英国的霸权。但实际上，这次会面并没有那么糟。

70
00:34:52.000 --> 00:35:11.000
NASA was quite impressed by Eden. He's got a little bit of the kind of cultural cringe. He says to his aide, Mohamed Heichael afterwards, what elegance. It was made to look as if we were beggars and they were princes. But they part on fairly decent terms and NASA actually says to Eden, you know, I'd actually quite like to come to Britain.
纳赛尔对艾登印象相当不错。他多多少少有点文化自卑感。他事后对他的助手穆罕默德·海卡尔说，多么优雅啊。这让人感觉仿佛我们是乞丐，而他们是王子。但他们以相当不错的氛围分手了，纳赛尔甚至对艾登说，你知道的，我其实很想去英国。

71
00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:27.000
Never been to Britain, like to come in a state visit, maybe in the autumn. But this visit never happens because after this thing starts to go wrong. So first of all, four days after their meeting, Britain signs this Baghdad pact. And NASA doesn't like this at all.
我从未去过英国，希望能进行一次国事访问，也许在秋天。但这次访问从未发生，因为在此之后事情开始出现问题。首先，在他们会面四天后，英国签署了这份巴格达条约。纳赛尔对此极其不满。

72
00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:56.000
He denounces it and he gets the voice of the Arab radio station, which is broadcast across the Middle East, to denounce it. The British are very upset by this. And so they back away from a deal to sell NASA some arms. NASA has been desperate for weapons to build up Egypt's army on their borders with Israel, to whom, of course, they'd lost a war at the end of the 1940s. And the British say, no, we're not going to sell in these arms, actually, because you won't join the Baghdad pact.
他对此予以谴责，并让整个中东地区都能收听到的“阿拉伯之声”广播电台去谴责它。英国人对此感到非常沮丧。因此，他们打消了向纳赛尔出售武器的交易。纳赛尔一直迫切需要武器来扩充埃及军队，以防范与以色列接壤的边境——在20世纪40年代末的战争中，他们输给了以色列。英国人说，不，我们实际上不会向你们出售这些武器，因为你们不肯加入巴格达条约。

73
00:35:56.000 --> 00:36:24.000
So at this point, NASA sends some feelers to Moscow. and the Kremlin are only too happy to organise some arms sales. So in September 1955, NASA announces to his great glee that he's signed an arms deal with Czechoslovakia. They're going to send him loads of tanks and planes and rocket launchers and all this. And in the West, this is perceived as a very worrying development in the Cold War. And actually, Eden thinks to himself, is NASA a communist? And he writes to President Eisenhower,
所以在这个时候，纳赛尔向莫斯科伸出了触角。而克里姆林宫则非常乐意组织一些武器销售。因此在1955年9月，纳赛尔兴高采烈地宣布他与捷克斯洛伐克签署了一项武器交易。他们将向他运送大量的坦克、飞机、火箭发射器以及所有这些装备。在西方，这被视为冷战中一个非常令人担忧的发展。实际上，艾登心里在想，纳赛尔是个共产主义者吗？他写信给艾森豪威尔总统，

74
00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:51.000
I suspect NASA's relations with the Soviet Union are a good deal closer than he admits. So actually he's in a complete muddle about whether NASA is a fascist or a communist. He's just a bad man. He's a evil dictator. It's probably a bit of both. Now, for Eden, the final straw comes the following spring. And I'm sorry to say, the Arabs behave very poorly to a product of our great British boarding schools. So one of Britain's closest allies in the Middle East is Jordan.
我怀疑纳赛尔与苏联的关系比他承认的要密切得多。因此，实际上他对于纳赛尔究竟是法西斯主义者还是共产主义者完全陷入了混乱。他只是个坏人。他是个邪恶的独裁者。可能两者兼而有之。现在，对艾登来说，最后一根稻草在第二年春天到来。我很遗憾地看到，阿拉伯人对我们伟大英国寄宿学校的产物表现得非常糟糕。因此，英国在中东最亲密的盟友之一是约旦。

75
00:36:51.000 --> 00:37:16.000
So we're very close to Jordan because we kind of installed the Hashemite dynasty on the throne of Jordan. Our army is called the Arab Legion. and it's commanded by Sir John Bagot Glob, who was a product of Cheltenham College. And he was called Glob Pasha. So everyone knows. Glob Pasha, you know, he runs the Jordanian army. He's basically the sort of boss of Jordan behind the scenes. Jordan has a new king in King Hussein, who's only 20.
所以我们与约旦非常亲密，因为我们在约旦王位上扶植了哈希姆王朝。我们的军队被称为阿拉伯军团。它由切尔滕纳姆学院毕业的约翰·巴戈特·格拉布爵士指挥。他被称为格拉布帕夏。所以大家都知道。格拉布帕夏，你知道的，他掌控着约旦军队。他基本上是约旦在幕后的老板。约旦迎来了新国王侯赛因国王，他年仅20岁。

76
00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:39.000
And King Hussein is coming under pressure from Arab nationalists himself, and he decides, I don't want people to think of me as a British puppet. So on the 1st of March, 1956, he sacks Globatch. and all the senior British officers in his army. And in London, this is a tremendous shock. And everybody says, God, this is dreadful. This is the end of our influence in the Middle East. This must be NASA's doing.
侯赛因国王自己也面临来自阿拉伯民族主义者的压力，他决定，我不想让人们认为我是英国的傀儡。因此，在1956年3月1日，他解雇了格拉布帕夏以及他军队中的所有高级英国军官。在伦敦，这引发了巨大的震撼。每个人都说，天哪，这太可怕了。这是我们在中东影响力的终结。这一定纳赛尔搞的鬼。

77
00:37:39.000 --> 00:38:11.000
It's actually not NASA's doing at all, but he makes a scapego for it. And Eden, by this point, he's taking all this really personally. So for him, NASA has become the personification of what clearly is this inevitable process, which is Britain's retreat from its overseas empire. And Eden, for obvious reasons which we've already discussed, he sees this through the lens of his own experiences. So he is a really good example of somebody who's a prisoner of his past.
这实际上根本不是纳赛尔干的，但他成了替罪羊。到这个时候，艾登把这一切都当成了针对他个人的事。对他而言，纳赛尔已经成为这个显而易见且不可逆转的过程——即英国从海外帝国撤退——的化身。出于我们已经讨论过的显而易见的原因，艾登通过他自身的经历来看待这一切。因此，他是一个典型的被过去所囚禁的人。

78
00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:56.000
He sees everything through the lens of the 1930s, appeasement, Mussolini, Hitler, Spain, Abyssinia, all of this. So as early as March 56, he's telling President Eisenhower, our policy of appeasement will bring us nothing in Egypt. We must do our own. utmost contain Egyptian policy. So basically what's got stuck in Eden's head is the idea that NASA is the new Mussolini and he must be contained and resisted and not appeased. So there's that and then I think there's another element to all this which is that Eden is also running into trouble domestically. So things haven't gone brilliantly since he became prime minister. Prices are rising too high. The pound is under pressure. He's got tense relations with his
他透过20世纪30年代的有色眼镜看待一切：绥靖政策、墨索里尼、希特勒、西班牙、阿比西尼亚，诸如此类。因此，早在56年3月，他就对艾森豪威尔总统说，我们的绥靖政策在埃及不会给我们带来任何好处。我们必须竭尽全力遏制埃及的政策。所以基本上，艾登脑子里固有的想法是，纳赛尔就是新墨索里尼，必须遏制和抵抗他，而不能对其实施绥靖。这就是情况所在，然后我认为这里面还有另一个因素，那就是艾登在国内也遇到了麻烦。自从他担任首相以来，事情并没有进展得十分顺利。物价上涨过快。英镑面临压力。他和他的

79
00:38:56.000 --> 00:39:32.000
Tory ministers. So in particular, these two guys, Rab Butler and Harold McMillan, who will talk about a bit later, they've both got ambitions at their own. There's a sort of sense, I think, that Eden is a bit past his best, that he's one of these people who waits for ages to become Prime Minister. And then when he gets the job, he's not quite as good as everybody thought he would be. A bit shop-soiled. A bit shop-souled. So Harold McMillan, who's a great man for a witty aphorism, said, well, the trouble with Anthony was that he was trained to win the Derby in 1938, but unfortunately he wasn't out of the starting stalls until 1955. Fabulous.
保守党大臣们的关系很紧张。特别是这两个家伙，拉布·巴特勒和哈罗德·麦克米伦，我们稍后会讨论他们，他们俩都有各自的野心。我认为有一种感觉，那就是艾登有点过了巅峰期，他是那种等了许久才当上首相的人。然后当他得到了这份工作时，他并没有人们想象的那么优秀。有点像积压货。有点像积压货。因此，善于发表妙语连珠言论的哈罗德·麦克米伦说，好吧，安东尼的问题在于，他是在1938年接受培训去赢得德比赛的，但不幸的是，他直到1955年才从起跑栏里出来。太棒了。

80
00:39:32.000 --> 00:40:01.000
Because he's not clubbable, he doesn't hang out with the backbenchers, he's not good at shaking hands in the House of Commons. I think a lot of people think that he's basically too elegant and cultured. He's a bit too smooth. I actually think there's a sort of, this is a bit like, what, I don't know, people on the right of the Tory party used to think about David Cameron. You know, it's all a bit too effortless and a little bit too smooth and shiny. Effortless superiority, which always goes down well with state agents in Essex.
因为他不合群，不和后座议员混在一起，他不擅长在下议院握手。我认为很多人认为他基本上过于优雅和文雅。他有点太油滑了。我实际上认为有那么一种，这有点像，什么，我不知道，保守党右翼的人以前对大卫·卡梅伦的看法。你知道的，一切都显得太毫不费力，也太圆滑光鲜了。毫不费力的优越感，这在埃塞克斯郡的房地产经纪人中总是很吃香。

81
00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:26.000
Right. So the Telegraph ran an editorial about Eden in 1956, which ended up sort of creating a phrase. They said that Eden had a gesture that what he would do is, when he was making a speech, he would punch his fist into his other palm, the emphasis. But he never quite followed through, so you never heard the blow. You never heard the smack. They want a smack. They want the firm smack of government.
没错。《电讯报》在1956年发表了一篇关于艾登的社论，最终创造了一个短语。他们说艾登有一个习惯动作，当他在发表演讲时，他会把拳头砸进另一只手掌里来表示强调。但他从未真正贯彻到底，所以你从未听到那记打击。你从未听到那记重击。他们想要一记重击。他们想要政府坚定有力的重击。

82
00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:55.000
Readers of the Telegraph love getting a smack. They do. The Telegraph wanted a smack and they weren't getting one. So, Eden is very sensitive and he finds all this enraging and upsetting. And he's even more enraged that he's being frustrated by this jumped-up Egyptian colonel, who's, you know, Eden probably thinks that Nassah Arabic is not as good as his. And on the 12th of March, a famous incident for people who read about the Suez Crisis, He gets a memo from a junior foreign office minister, protégé of his called Anthony Nutting.
《电讯报》的读者喜欢挨一记重击。他们确实喜欢。《电讯报》想要一记重击，但他们没有得到。因此，艾登非常敏感，他发现所有这一切都令人愤怒和沮丧。而更让他愤怒的是，他竟然被这个暴发户般的埃及上校给挫败了，你知道的，艾登可能认为纳赛尔的阿拉伯语没有他好。在3月12日，对于阅读苏伊士运河危机历史的人来说是一个著名的日子，他收到了外交部一名初级大臣、他的门生安东尼·纳丁的一份备忘录。

83
00:40:55.000 --> 00:41:12.000
Nothing. Nothing. Yeah. Unbelievable. Nothing says, I feel that we can isolate Egypt by building bridges with other Middle Eastern countries. And Nutting that evening is having dinner at the Savoy. Of course. When he's summoned to the phone, telephone call for you, sir, the prime minister.
纳丁。纳丁。是的。难以置信。纳丁说，我觉得我们可以通过与中东其他国家建立桥梁来孤立埃及。那天晚上，纳丁正在萨沃伊酒店吃晚餐。理所当然。这时他被叫去接电话，有您的电话，先生，是首相打来的。

84
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:35.000
And Eden is on the phone. And Eden shouts at him down the phone. He says, what's all this nonsense about isolating NASA or neutralizing him, as you call it? I want him destroyed, can't you understand? And Nutting tries to calm him down and says, look, you know, I don't think we should be destroying NASA. I think we should, you know, we should try to find an alternative. The only result of removing NASA would be anarchy in Egypt.
艾登在电话那头。艾登对着电话大喊。他说，孤立纳赛尔或者像你所说的那样中和他的这番废话是怎么回事？我要他毁灭，你难道不明白吗？纳丁试图让他冷静下来并说，听着，你知道的，我认为我们不应该毁灭纳赛尔。我认为我们应该，你知道的，我们应该试着寻找一个替代方案。罢免纳赛尔的唯一结果只能是埃及的无政府状态。

85
00:41:35.000 --> 00:42:10.000
And Eden just shouts at him down the phone, I don't give a damn if there's anarchy and chaos in Egypt. And then he slams down the phone. So as Nutting said, from that point onwards, Eden completely lost his touch, driven by the impulses of pride and prestige and nagged by mounting sickness, he began to behave like an enraged elephant charging senselessly at invisible and imaginary enemies in the international jungle. Now, NASA is not behaving like an element, he's behaving like a panther. So on the 13th of June, the last British troops, as per the deal, leave Port Said.
艾登对着电话大喊大叫，我才不管埃及有没有无政府状态和混乱呢。然后他狠狠地挂断了电话。正如纳丁所说，从那一刻起，艾登彻底失去了理智。在骄傲和虚荣心的驱使下，又饱受不断恶化的病痛折磨，他开始像一头愤怒的大象，在国际丛林中盲目地冲撞着那些看不见的、虚构的敌人。现在，纳赛尔可不是一头大象，他表现得像一只黑豹。因此，在6月13日，最后一批英国军队按照协议离开了塞得港。

86
00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:29.000
They leave the canal's home. And NASA declares a three-day public holiday. He says this is brilliant. Egypt is no longer under the domination of the imperialists. for entering a bright new era, all of this. Ten days later, they hold presidential elections in Egypt. NASA, I have to say, does incredibly well. I mean, he really does do well.
他们撤出了运河区。纳赛尔宣布放假三天。他说这太棒了。埃及不再受帝国主义者的统治了。我们正在迎来一个光明的全新时代，诸如此类。十天后，他们在埃及举行了总统选举。我不得不说，纳赛尔表现得好极了。我的意思是，他确实表现得很好。

87
00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:47.000
He wins 99% of the vote. Are there any other candidates? There are no other candidates. So the question, which surely would be passing everybody, what happened to the other 1%? I think that's just spoiled ballots or something. Who knows? People have let themselves down.
他赢得了99%的选票。还有其他候选人吗？没有其他候选人。所以这个问题肯定会在每个人脑海中闪现：那剩下的1%发生了什么？我想那只是废票之类的东西。谁知道呢。总有人表现得让人失望。

88
00:42:47.000 --> 00:43:08.000
So NASA has promised the Egyptian people this great new age of national renewal, public works, modernisation, welfare, education, all this. And what's going to lie behind all this is this great new project, the Aswan High Dam. Oh, terrible for archaeology. They moved, like, what did they move? They moved the statue of Ramesses II.
因此，纳赛尔向埃及人民承诺了这个国家革新、公共工程、现代化、福利、教育等各个方面的伟大新时代。而这一切背后的支撑将是这个伟大的全新项目——阿斯旺高坝。哦，对考古学来说太糟糕了。他们搬迁了，比如，他们搬迁了什么？他们搬迁了拉美西斯二世的雕像。

89
00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:23.000
Yeah. Abysimble. Abusimble, exactly. But it just looked brilliant by the Nile. It doesn't look as good now. But I'm aware that, you know, obviously the electrification of Egypt is possibly more important. Yeah. So this was at the time, the most ambitious engineering project in the world.
是的。阿布辛拜勒。正是阿布辛拜勒。但这在尼罗河畔看起来确实棒极了。现在看起来就没那么好了。但我知道，显而易见，埃及的电气化可能更为重要。是的。所以这在当时是世界上最具雄心的工程项目。

90
00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:55.000
They were going to build this three mile wide dam, 365 feet high. It's 17 times taller from the Great Pyramid of Giza. Nice fact for you. It will create this reservoir, Lake NASA, which will store all this water. They will be able to regulate the Nile. They will reclaim millions of acres for irrigation and for farming. they will produce colossal quantities of electricity. You know, this dam is going to transform Egypt economically. But it's incredibly expensive. And NASA is going to pay for it with a loan that he's got.
他们要修建这座三英里宽、365英尺高的水坝。它比吉萨大金字塔高17倍。告诉你一个冷知识。它将形成这个水库——纳赛尔湖，用来储存所有这些水。他们将能够调节尼罗河。他们将为灌溉和耕作开垦数百万英亩的土地。他们将产生巨大的电力。你知道的，这座水坝将在经济上彻底改变埃及。但它的造价极其昂贵。而纳赛尔将用他获得的贷款来支付这笔费用。

91
00:43:55.000 --> 00:44:18.000
He's got loans basically from the USA, from Britain, and from the World Bank for a total of $270 million. Anyway, a month after his election, NASA gets a terrible shock. The American Secretary of State, John Foster Dulles, on the 19th of July, calls in the Egyptian ambassador and says, the deal is off. American public opinion has turned against you.
他基本上从美国、英国和世界银行获得了总计2.7亿美元的贷款。总之，在他当选一个月后，纳赛尔遭遇了一个可怕的打击。美国国务卿约翰·福斯特·杜勒斯在7月19日召见了埃及大使，并宣布交易取消。美国公众舆论已经转向反对你们。

92
00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:32.000
You know, maybe you are a communist. And actually, this is the most insulting thing and is actually wrong. We don't think the Egyptian people are capable of this, but you know, you're just Egyptians. You're never going to be able to build this down. I mean, an American saying that to the people who built the pyramids.
你知道的，也许你真的是个共产主义者。事实上，这是最具侮辱性且完全错误的事情。我们不认为埃及人民有能力做到这一点，但你知道的，你们只是埃及人。你们永远也建不成这座水坝。我的意思是，一个美国人竟然对建造了金字塔的人民说出这样的话。

93
